WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.192 --> 00:00:03.610
(crowd cheering)
(applause)

2
00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:06.320
Thank you so much, welcome back!

3
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:09.330
This hour we are celebrating the extraordinary nurses

4
00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:12.040
who care for us during the toughest of times.

5
00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.170
And our next guest is no exception.

6
00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:16.320
She is a nurse, innovator and advocate

7
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.380
for her fellow nurses, addressing health disparities

8
00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:23.380
for underserved people, and she is truly amazing.

9
00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:27.291
So let's take a peak at Andrea's story.

10
00:00:27.291 --> 00:00:30.670
(uplifting music)

11
00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:34.570 line:15%
Hi, my name is Andrea Jaramillo and I am a hospice nurse,

12
00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:36.970
a clinical research nurse, a consultant

13
00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:39.860
for a wellness startup, and a nurse farmer.

14
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.800
I was born in Ecuador and grew up never knowing

15
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.130
a single nurse.

16
00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:48.430
I moved to the US when I was 16 and became an interpreter

17
00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:51.380
for the National Institute of Health.

18
00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:55.000
It was there where I saw the strength and power of nurses

19
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.750
in a patient's life, and I knew that I wanted to care

20
00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.610
for people in the same way.

21
00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:02.860
Working with underserved communities is very important

22
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:06.690
for me because I was one of the patients who did not

23
00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.870
have access to care at one point, so giving back

24
00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:13.240
to the community, it's very rewarding for me.

25
00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:17.110
Nurses are the cornerstone of every healthcare system,

26
00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:20.830
and immigrants are the backbone of American society.

27
00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:24.870
I think of nurses as activists, advocates,

28
00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:28.170
but most importantly, as catalyst for change.

29
00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:32.220
That's why I always felt compelled to bring my own chair

30
00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.410
to the table.

31
00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:35.920
After years of being a registered nurse,

32
00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.970
I realized how quickly nurses could burn out,

33
00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:43.500
which was exacerbated even more with the onset of COVID.

34
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.840
Nurses are natural problem solvers, and because we spend

35
00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.610
so much time caring for patients directly,

36
00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:53.190
we're always seeing ways to improve our systems of care,

37
00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:56.600
but we get frustrated, because for many nurses,

38
00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.530
there is no clear way to raise and implement

39
00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:02.130
these amazing ideas, and we want to be a part

40
00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.230
of the solution.

41
00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:05.280
That is why I became involved

42
00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.303
in the Society Of Nurse Scientists, Innovators,

43
00:02:09.303 --> 00:02:12.900
Entrepreneurs, and Leaders, or SONSIEL for short.

44
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.290
There we provide a community for nurses

45
00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:17.700
with innovative ideas to come together

46
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.140
and support each other.

47
00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.940
We want to empower nurses to make an impact

48
00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:25.290
as the innovators and pioneers that we are.

49
00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:29.230
SONSIEL has given me permission to define what nursing means

50
00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:33.090
to me, and allow me to rediscover my love for caring

51
00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.610
for others without losing myself.

52
00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:39.323
I want to continue to help nurses discover the same thing.

53
00:02:40.843 --> 00:02:41.913
(applause)
(crowd cheering)

54
00:02:41.913 --> 00:02:43.815
Please everybody, give a warm welcome to nurse

55
00:02:43.815 --> 00:02:45.232
Andrea Jaramillo!

56
00:02:46.953 --> 00:02:48.637
Hi, thank you.

57
00:02:48.637 --> 00:02:52.000
I am so excited to celebrate you.

58
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.520
Okay, so you worked very hard to become a nurse.

59
00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:56.830
Is this correct?

60
00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:57.663
This is correct.

61
00:02:57.663 --> 00:02:59.850
It took me about 10 years.

62
00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:02.230
You know, I applied to almost all the schools

63
00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:03.730 line:15%
in the New England area.

64
00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:07.460 line:15%
I had seven rejections and eventually I got into this

65
00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:09.860 line:15%
really great university, UMass Boston,

66
00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:13.232
where I graduated on 2017 with honors.

67
00:03:13.232 --> 00:03:15.482
(applause)

68
00:03:18.353 --> 00:03:19.186
How wonderful.

69
00:03:19.186 --> 00:03:22.340
Well you can show them, "See, you accepted me,

70
00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:23.997
and look what I did"

71
00:03:23.997 --> 00:03:27.010
And SONSIEL was started in 2019,

72
00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:30.010
but you and your team have already been

73
00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:32.702
to the United Nations?
Yes. (chuckling)

74
00:03:32.702 --> 00:03:34.010
We did.

75
00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:36.970
We got a team of nurses, a group,

76
00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:40.360 line:15%
and we showed up to learn how we can be part

77
00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.810 line:15%
of these committees where decisions are being made.

78
00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:45.470
Nurses, we are experts.

79
00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:48.060
We need to bring our expertise to the table.

80
00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:52.480
We need to bring our voices, and I think there's 28 million

81
00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:55.160
nurses, roughly, around the world,

82
00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:58.510
and we are underrepresented where decisions are being made.

83
00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:01.530
So I think this is the main thing we want to bring,

84
00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:04.720
we want to bring our chair and sit at those tables

85
00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.570
because if we're not, you know, at the table,

86
00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:10.255
we're on the menu, and I think it's important.

87
00:04:10.255 --> 00:04:14.740
(Drew sighs)
(applause)

88
00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:17.840
Because we are teaming up with Johnson &amp; Johnson

89
00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:22.100 line:15%
to honor nurses, I wanted to invite a friend,

90
00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:24.730 line:15%
a new friend of mine, to join us.

91
00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:26.770 line:15%
Please welcome Johnson and Johnson

92
00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:30.409 line:15%
executive vice president, Michael Sneed.

93
00:04:30.409 --> 00:04:33.208
(crowd cheers)
(applause)

94
00:04:33.208 --> 00:04:34.218
Hello, Michael.

95
00:04:34.218 --> 00:04:35.051
Hello!

96
00:04:36.791 --> 00:04:38.071
Good to see you.

97
00:04:38.071 --> 00:04:40.151
I love your jacket.

98
00:04:40.151 --> 00:04:43.721
(crowd cheering)
(applause)

99
00:04:43.721 --> 00:04:45.492
This is fantastic.

100
00:04:45.492 --> 00:04:46.440
This is fantastic.

101
00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.170
So much of what you've done has made this possible,

102
00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:50.230
really and truly.

103
00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:52.460
We're indebted to you with gratitude

104
00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:55.720
that is honestly not able to be put into words,

105
00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:59.260
and what I'd like to ask you is, you know,

106
00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:03.070
you've decided to make it a company mission and a priority

107
00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:06.943
to advocate for support and to empower nurses.

108
00:05:06.943 --> 00:05:09.520
Why did that come about?

109
00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.897
What is it that spoke to you and why did you say,

110
00:05:12.897 --> 00:05:14.690
"This is what we need to do"?

111
00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:17.670
Well look, Johnson &amp; Johnson, it's certainly a priority

112
00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:20.530 line:15%
for us when it comes to nursing, but Drew,

113
00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:22.830 line:15%
it's also a personal priority for me.

114
00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:25.200 line:15%
Over the last several months, I've actually traveled

115
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.900 line:15%
the country, talking to nurses in communities

116
00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:30.460
and really hearing their stories.

117
00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:34.980
And it's been tough, you know, the nurses around the US

118
00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:37.340
have been dealing with the pandemic.

119
00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:40.920
They've been trying to work to close the health gap

120
00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:42.420
in their communities.

121
00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:45.930
And oftentimes they're feeling burned out,

122
00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:50.600
they're feeling neglected, as Andrea talked about,

123
00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:54.290
but at the same time, I've heard some great stories

124
00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:56.060
that have been so uplifting.

125
00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:58.670
You know, when I see these nurses and I hear their stories,

126
00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:01.470
what I can tell you about nurses is that they are

127
00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:03.263
problem solvers.

128
00:06:03.263 --> 00:06:07.340
I can tell you that they are advocates for their patients.

129
00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:09.540
I can tell you that they're innovators.

130
00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:11.700
I can tell you that they are leaders in the community.

131
00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.850
Nurses are the backbone of the healthcare system,

132
00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:16.870
and so when we lift them up,

133
00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:19.480
it's better for us, it's better for all of us,

134
00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:21.120
and certainly better for our health.

135
00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:24.230 line:15%
And it's one of the reasons why Johnson &amp; Johnson

136
00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:28.310 line:15%
has been such a supporter of nurses for the last 125 years.

137
00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:32.740 line:15%
And it's because we see their ability to really

138
00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:37.740
be advocates, to have the ability to empower themselves

139
00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:41.080
and each other, to be able to educate people,

140
00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:43.080
and when you think about superheroes,

141
00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:44.570
that's what superheros do.

142
00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:47.664
And so that's what all these nurses do every single day.

143
00:06:47.664 --> 00:06:50.235
(applause)

144
00:06:50.235 --> 00:06:51.430
Now, I think you might have a little surprise for Andrea.

145
00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:57.040
Is this correct?

146
00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:00.880
As you know, you talked about her organization, SONSIEL,

147
00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:02.780
which is a tremendous organization, by the way,

148
00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:04.630
doing amazing things.

149
00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:07.400
But the great thing about it is that SONSIEL

150
00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:10.950
is an organization that empowers so many other nurses

151
00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:14.020
when it comes to what they do in terms of being a nurse.

152
00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:17.170
They're scientists and all the things that they do.

153
00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:19.990
So on behalf of Johnson &amp; Johnson,

154
00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:24.153
we want to present to you a grant of $25,000

155
00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:28.844
to help you continue the work that you do.

156
00:07:28.844 --> 00:07:31.094
(applause)

157
00:07:33.113 --> 00:07:38.113
(crowd cheering)
(applause continues)

158
00:07:38.357 --> 00:07:39.860
I'd love to get in on this.

159
00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:41.960
I'm also gonna do a little something for you,

160
00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:45.240
because again, all I have to offer, all I make

161
00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:50.240
are things that, you know, I want to improve life with

162
00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:52.410
and spark joy.

163
00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:54.160
So I would also like to send you

164
00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:56.910
with all the beautiful appliances,

165
00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:59.410
the Flower hair tools and the Flower beauty,

166
00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:02.350
as a woman to woman gift.

167
00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:06.470
(crowd cheers)
(applause)

168
00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:07.740
It's amazing, thank you, Drew.

169
00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:08.573
Well, thank you.

170
00:08:08.573 --> 00:08:11.100
Andrea Jaramillo, and thank you

171
00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:16.010
to the executive vice president of Johnson &amp; Johnson,

172
00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:17.960
Michael Sneed.

173
00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:20.640
What you do for the world is incredible,

174
00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:23.830
and thank you for being a part of this show and this hour.

175
00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:24.770
We will be right back.

176
00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:26.050
Thank you so much.

177
00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:29.817 line:15%
(upbeat music)

